WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.490
Book Four, Proposition Four.

2
00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:05.522
In a given triangle to inscribe a circle.

3
00:00:05.522 --> 00:00:12.250
Let A B C be the given triangle; it is required to inscribe a circle in the triangle A B C.

4
00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:22.881
Bisect the angles A B C and A C B by the straight lines B D and C D by One Nine, and let them meet one another at the point D.

5
00:00:22.881 --> 00:00:29.040
From D, draw D E, D F, and D G perpendicular to the sides A B, B C, and C A.

6
00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:46.619
Because angle A B D equals angle C B D, the right angle B E D equals the right angle B F D, and the hypotenuse B D is common, by One Twenty-Six the triangles E B D and F B D have their remaining sides equal, so D E equals D F.

7
00:00:46.619 --> 00:00:55.730
For the same reason, D G also equals D F; therefore all three perpendiculars D E, D F, and D G are equal to one another.

8
00:00:55.730 --> 00:01:19.190
Hence the circle described with center D and distance D E passes through F and G, and touches A B, B C, and C A because the angles at E, F, and G are right—for if it cut them, a line drawn at right angles to a diameter from its extremity would fall within the circle, which was proved absurd in Three Sixteen.

9
00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:27.466
Let the circle E F G be described; therefore in the given triangle A B C the circle E F G has been inscribed.

10
00:01:27.466 --> 00:01:29.060
Which was to be done.

11
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:33.781
Proposition Four Four constructs the incenter and incircle of any triangle.

12
00:01:33.781 --> 00:01:47.886
Because D E equals D G, joining D A proves that all three internal angle bisectors concur at D; and as Heath notes, bisecting the exterior supplementary angles yields the three escribed circles, giving four circles tangent to the three sidelines.

13
00:01:47.886 --> 00:02:02.221
In the Metrica, Heron of Alexandria used this exact diagram to decompose the triangle into three pairs of congruent right triangles around the incenter D, proving that the area equals the semiperimeter times the inradius and deriving Heron's formula.

14
00:02:02.221 --> 00:02:14.320
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred fifty-four unites fourteen direct prerequisites across fifty ancestor nodes, linking Book One angle bisection and Angle-Angle-Side congruence with Book Three tangency.

15
00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:27.700
